---
title: "SOR iteration for sparse matrices"
author: "Ožbej Kresal"
date: "June 2026"
---

In this exercise we implemented a sparse matrix datatype RedkaMatrika and the necessary interfaces to use the datatype to solve a $Ax = b$ system using SOR iteration. We then tested our sparse matrix and SOR implementation on two graphs, where we used the physical method for graph embedding to embed them in a plane.

# Sparse matrix implementation
We defined a new data type RedkaMatrika, which stores the non-zero elements of the matrix due to space requirements in two double arrays V and I, such that:

$$
V[i][j] = a_{i,\,I[i][j]}
$$

Therefore, the array $V$ contains the non-zero elements of the matrix $A$, whereas the array I contains
the column indices. The array $V[i]$ thus contains the non-zero elements from the $i$-th row of matrix
$A$, and the array $I[i]$ stores the column indices of these non-zero elements in the same order.

``\\``
Example:
$$
A =
\begin{bmatrix}
10 & 0 & 2 \\
0 & 5 & 0 \\
3 & 0 & 8
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\text{V} =
\begin{bmatrix}
10 & 2 \\
5 \\
3 & 8
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\text{I} =
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 3 \\
2 \\
1 & 3
\end{bmatrix}.
$$

## Operations
We then implemented the following operations:
- **`Base.size`**  
  Returns the dimensions of the sparse matrix.

- **`Base.getindex`**  
  Returns the value at position `(i,j)`. If the element is not stored, it is assumed to be zero.

- **`Base.setindex!`**  
  Updates the value at position `(i,j)` and modifies the sparse representation accordingly.

- **`Base.*`**  
  Implements matrix vector multiplication using only the stored non-zero elements, making the operation more efficient than with a dense matrix.

## SOR iteration to solve sparse linear systems
To solve sparse systems $Ax = b$ we implemented a SOR iteration function adapted to work with our sparse matrix datatype:
$$
x,\; it = \mathrm{sor}(A,b,x_0,\omega,\texttt{tol}=10^{-10}),
$$

where $x_0$ is the initial approximation, $\texttt{tol}$ is the condition for stopping the iteration, and $\omega$ is the parameter in the SOR iteration. The iteration then stops when
$$
\|Ax^{(k)}-b\|_{\infty} < \delta,
$$
where $\delta$ is given by the $\texttt{tol}$ argument.

By adapting the method to our sparse matrix implementation, we effectively reduce the computational cost of a single iteration from $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ to $\mathcal{O}(\text{number of non-zero elements})$, which greatly increases the speed of computation for sparse matrices.

# Demonstration - embedding graphs

To demonstrate our implementation we will embed two graphs in a plane using the physical method, which we adapted to work with our sparse matrix SOR implementation. 

## Cyclic ladder graph
Unembedded graph:
```julia, echo=false
using dn1, Graphs, GraphRecipes, Plots

function lestev(omega)
    G = krožna_lestev(8)
    t = range(0, 2pi, 9)[1:end-1]
    x = cos.(t)
    y = sin.(t)
    točke = hcat(hcat(x, y)', zeros(2, 8))
    # funkcija hcat zloži vektorje po stolpcih v matriko
    fix = 1:8
    it = vloži!(G, fix, točke, omega)
    return it, G, točke
end

function grid(omega)
    m, n = 6, 6
    G = Graphs.grid((m, n), periodic=false)
    rob = filter(v -> degree(G, v) <= 3, vertices(G))
    urejen_rob = [rob[1]]
    # uredi točke na robu v cikel
    for i = 1:length(rob)-1
        sosedi = neighbors(G, urejen_rob[end])
        sosedi = intersect(sosedi, rob)
        sosedi = setdiff(sosedi, urejen_rob)
        push!(urejen_rob, sosedi[1])
    end
    t = range(0, 2pi, length(rob) + 1)[1:end-1]
    točke = zeros(2, n * m)
    točke[:, urejen_rob] = hcat(cos.(t), sin.(t))'
    it = vloži!(G, urejen_rob, točke, omega)
    return it, G, točke
end
omegas = 0.1:0.01:1.9
iterations = [lestev(omega)[1] for omega in omegas]

G = krožna_lestev(8)
display(graphplot(G, curves=false))
```

Embedded graph using the physical method with our SOR implementation:
```julia, echo=false
best_omega = omegas[argmin(iterations)]
_, G, točke = lestev(best_omega)
display(graphplot(G, x=točke[1, :], y=točke[2, :], curves=false))
```

Effect of $\omega$ on SOR convergence:  
```julia, echo=false
display(plot(omegas, iterations, xlabel="\\omega", ylabel="SOR iterations"; legend=false))
println("Best omega for ladder: $best_omega")
```

## Grid graph
Unembedded graph:
```julia, echo=false
omegas = 0.1:0.01:1.9
iterations = [grid(omega)[1] for omega in omegas]
G = Graphs.grid((6, 6), periodic=false)
display(graphplot(G, curves=false))
```

Embedded graph using the physical method with our SOR implementation, with some points fixed in a circle:
```julia, echo=false
best_omega = omegas[argmin(iterations)]
_, G, točke = grid(best_omega)
display(graphplot(G, x=točke[1, :], y=točke[2, :], curves=false))
```

Effect of $\omega$ on SOR convergence:  
```julia, echo=false

display(plot(omegas, iterations, xlabel="\\omega", ylabel="SOR iterations"; legend=false))
println("Best omega for grid: $best_omega")
```









